// @no-instantiate
// MISCOMPILE, pinned: an ANONYMOUS `{ r: i32 }` object literal bound to an UN-ANNOTATED local
// and returned from a function whose RETURN is annotated `Circle[]`, in a module that also
// declares a standalone struct of that arm's exact layout (`Dot`). Eleven lines, no import, no
// generic, no lambda. `vl check` exits 0 with NO diagnostics at all — not even a hint — and is
// the ONLY command that misses it; `vl check --codegen` exits 1, `vl build` writes the `.wasm`
// and exits 1, `vl run` exits 1:
//
//   the emitted module is not valid wasm … type mismatch:
//   expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)
//
// (No byte offset quoted on purpose: it moves with the seed. The message is the stable
// identifier.)
//
// THE RETURN ANNOTATION IS THE TRIGGER, and that is what makes this a DIFFERENT rung from D36
// rather than the same one. Controls, each ONE line different from this file and each RUN:
//   · the return annotation DROPPED (`function mk(n: i32) {`)   → runs, prints 7  ← the axis
//   · `Dot` DELETED                                             → runs, prints 7
//   · `Sq` + `Shape` DELETED, so `Circle` is not an arm         → runs, prints 7
//   · `type Dot = { q: i32 }` (same arity, different field)     → runs, prints 7
//   · the LOCAL annotated (`const o: Circle[] = [{ r: n }]`)    → runs, prints 7
//
// So it needs three things at once: the anonymous element, the exact-layout standalone twin,
// and a `Circle[]` annotation on the RESULT but not on the local.
//
// WHAT IT IS. `arrLitElemName` resolves an anonymous object element to a nominal ROW, struct
// table first (`structIndexOfObj` → `Dot`) and the variant table second. That ladder is what
// D36/D38's fix installed and it matches every CONSUMER of an anonymous shape. Here a fourth
// party disagrees: the function's declared `Circle[]` result. The checker does not push that
// annotation into the un-annotated local — probed, the array node's recorded element is the
// anonymous `{r: i32}` even with the return annotation present — so the local builds `Dot`'s
// row while the result boundary says `uVarHeap[Circle]`. Delete `Dot` and the ladder's second
// rung answers `Circle`, which is what the boundary wanted, and the program runs.
//
// PRE-EXISTING, not a regression: silent on master `f2064bec` too, with the same `vl check` rc
// 0 and the same engine message. What the D36/D38 change moved is its BYTES, not its outcome —
// master built the union BOX for the element and this tree builds `Dot`'s row; both disagree
// with the boundary. It was 8 of the 900 cells in that change's grid (`anon` x `list` x the
// four ANNOTATED routes x the `exact` and `after` twin spellings), live at the same
// coordinates under both compilers.
//
// IT IS THE SPECIMEN. This file is kept byte-for-byte identical to
// `tests/vl_check_codegen_test.ts`'s `INVALID_MODULE_SRC` below its header, which needs a
// program that type-checks clean and whose module the engine refuses.
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED this file starts failing (the module instantiates), which is the point:
// delete it, graduate a `@run` row, and swap `INVALID_MODULE_SRC` for another live member of
// the class. READ THE NOTE IN `tests/vl_check_codegen_test.ts` BEFORE DOING THAT — including
// what it says about where to look before concluding the class is empty.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type Dot = { r: i32 }

function mk(n: i32): Circle[] {
  const o = [{ r: n }]
  return o
}

print(mk(7)[0].r)
